  - [1mThird-party analytics SDK[0m — Segment/PostHog/etc. own the ID. Most capability, but it's a vendor dependency and a
    much larger change.

  Cheap to add later: body and cookie, if the module owns the ID behind a small interface. Expensive: the SDK, which
  tends to want to own identity itself.

  I'd recommend [1mclient-side only[0m — with [38;5;153mlogin()[39m stubbed there's no real request to attach anything to, and keeping the
  module's surface small means sending it later is a one-line change at the call site.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What consumes the visitor ID once it exists? → Client-side only for now (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I present the design. The project has no configured tooling at all — no scripts, dependencies,
  linter, or test runner in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m — and the ES-module choice means it needs at least a static-server script to
  run the page. Setting tooling up is cheapest now, before there's code to retrofit.

  The visitor-ID module has genuinely testable logic (generate once, persist, return the same value on reload, survive
  corrupt storage), so unit tests would pay for themselves immediately. End-to-end and fuzz testing are harder to
  justify for a login stub.
